%!testlib
% Writing a binary object sequence must measure its size in a width the
% size cannot overflow, and must not walk a shared reference graph without
% end, run through `xpost -d null` by run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff
% both hold.
%
% printobject and writeobject measure the sequence, allocate a buffer that
% size, then write the objects into it. The measure counted records and
% bytes in a 32-bit field with no ceiling, and a program can make the
% measured size exceed 2^32 with a tiny graph by pointing many references
% at one large object: the field wraps, the buffer is allocated far too
% small, and the write runs off its end with bytes and a length the
% program chose -- a heap overflow from ordinary, unprivileged PostScript.
% The same measure walked the graph as though it were a tree, visiting a
% shared subtree once per reference, so a graph a few dozen nodes deep made
% it traverse billions of nodes and hang.
%
% Both are refused now: the size is measured against a ceiling far above
% any real sequence, and the walk ends at the ceiling too. Neither the
% overflow nor the endless walk should reach the interpreter; each is a
% limitcheck the measure raises before a buffer is ever sized.

/errname { % {proc} . name-or-null
    stopped { $error /errorname get }{ null } ifelse
} def

1 setobjectformat

% Many references to one large string: the measured data size is
% 2 * 65535 * 65535, which is more than 2^32.
/s 65535 string def
/L 65535 array def
0 1 65534 { L exch s put } for
{ [ L L ] 0 printobject } errname /limitcheck eq
    (a print whose measured size overflows the size width is refused) assert

% A doubling graph: each array holds two references to the one below, so a
% tree-walk of it visits 2^31 nodes.
/a [ 0 ] def
0 1 30 { pop /a [ a a ] def } for
{ a 0 printobject } errname /limitcheck eq
    (a print whose measure would walk a graph without end is refused) assert

verdict
